   "featured": true,
   "summary": "A large group research and design project for a custom made DIRC cherenekov radiation detector test at DESY.",
   "description": "This is probably the largest project I’ve done to date. This project was the culmination of 6 months of work to create a small Cherenkov detector that we tested at DESY for the Beamline for Schools competition. For this project me and group of peers made a custom PCB with silicon photomultipliers, a mounting system for the board to be put on remote translation stages, a dark box, a Monte-Carlo simulation for the expected path of photons, and multiple data analysis and graphing programs to reconstruct a cherenkov ring.",
   "learned": "From this project I had to teach myself how to make electronics schematics and use all the various functions of KiCad to make a PCB that was manufactured. I also had to learn how to work on the fly with Solidworks to quickly make prototypes, and functional yet simple models. I also had to spend a lot of time figuring out the vector math for photon tracing, as well as, rendering using Matplotlib.",
   "takeaway": "This has been to this date probably the biggest opportunity that has been afforded to me. Although I am not a physicist, I believe my group had the most successful results and data, and I was extremely relieved that we were able to detect a cherenkov ring. I was also proud to represent the United States well, and perform the best as the only public school in the competition…",
