   "featured": false,
   "summary": "A small space roguelike game made for the Playdate console using Lua in the Playdate SDK.",
   "description": "For this project, I created a top scrolling infinite shoot-em-up space game with resource, upgrade, and shop mechanics for the Playdate. The game has a unique control scheme in which the player holds the console sideways, and uses the Playdate’s crank as a steering wheel. For the art assets I used a custom dithering blender shader to create 2D sprites from 3D renders similar to games like Donkey Kong Country. Unfortunately I never finished this game due to lack of time, and scope creep.",
   "learned": "From this project, I learned a lot about how to use the Playdate SDK, and how to build games for physical hardware. I had to learn to keep art relatively large to be able to be seen on the 2.7 inch screen. I also have learned that gameplay should remain simple because of the limited amount of controls and screen real estate. Most importantly, I learned that small games like this one should remain simple in order to not lose sight of the scope, and ensure they can be finished.",
   "takeaway": "Despite not finishing the game, I think the art assets and the feel of the game are very fun. I think the aesthetic and basic gameplay are very enticing, and I think I could reuse some of these assets in the future. If I have the time to go back and revisit this project in the future, I would love to finish it. I hope to take forward what I have learned about scope creep, and try to ensure I stay realistic when working on future projects…",
