   "description": "For this project I used Solidworks to design a small handheld device that could launch 1 inch diameter laser cut wood disks. Because of the complexity of the design I had to create multiple CAD designs, then use Solidworks’ assembly feature to test how they went together. I then assembled the physical model after 3D printing, and was able to shoot the disks ~50 feet.",
   "learned": "During this project I learned about how to design a successful device using CAD. I also learned a lot about how to account for tolerance, and how designs need to have wiggle room for when the 3D printer filament expands. Additionally, I learned how to use Solidworks’ drawing file feature to create simple designs for the laser cutter.",
   "takeaway": "This project was really fun, and I feel the knowledge I gained about tolerances and the strength of 3D printing will really shape how I design future projects and designs. If I were to do this project again I would maybe use a rubber band to feed the magazine instead of gravity, and focus more on ergonomics…",
